---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The estate">
  <h1>The estate of Robert Paul Lerena</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A death certificate, a will, a liquidation account and a son's death notice — four documents that
    between them name the head of this family, his wife, and his three sons, and close the gap to
    David Defranceski's grandfather.
  </p>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.4rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> Estate of the late <strong>Robert Paul Lerena of Newlands,
      Cape</strong>, died <strong>19 March 1950</strong>. Letters of Administration
      <strong>No. 1847/50</strong> — the same file NAAIRS indexes as
      <span class="mono">KAB MOOC 6/9/17017</span>. Held in the family's papers; images preserved at
      <span class="mono">sources/estate-robert-paul-lerena/</span>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The death certificate</h2>
  <p>
    Union of South Africa death certificate <strong>A 214036</strong>, registered in the district of
    <strong>Wynberg</strong>, Province of the Cape of Good Hope, entry <span class="mono">W612/50</span>:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th style="width:12rem">Name</th><td><strong>Robert Paul LERINA</strong> <span class="small">— so
          spelled by the registrar; every other document in the file says <strong>Lerena</strong></span></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Age</th><td>76 years</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Birthplace</th><td><strong>South America</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Personal status</th><td>Widower</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Occupation</th><td><strong>Race Horse Owner</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><th>Date of death</th><td>Nineteenth March, 1950</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Place</th><td>7 Forth Road, Newlands, Wynberg district</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cause</th><td>Coronary thrombosis; angina pectoris — 6 weeks, about 3 years</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Doctor</th><td>J. R. E. Lee</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Burial</th><td><strong>Maitland Cemetery no 1</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Race Horse Owner.</strong> The family said he brought horses; the state, recording his death,
    gave that as his trade. Taken with the 1931 municipal file for
    <a href={u("/naairs")}>stables at Koeberg Road</a>, the horses are now documented from three separate
    directions. <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>

  <h2>Mary Septima</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> He was a widower because his wife had died fifteen years
    earlier. The <strong>Maitland Road Cemetery register of interments</strong>, page 3215, entry 163,
    November 1935:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Lerena, <strong>Mary Septima</strong>, female, <strong>51</strong>, of Observatory, 34 Florence Road.
    Roman Catholic allotment, Cemetery no. 1, grave <strong>7719 A</strong>, 6&prime;9&Prime; below surface.
    Father Graham officiating; undertaker L. Pitt.
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    She was about 44 when her youngest son was born and 51 when she died. The certifying doctor is
    <strong>J. R. E. Lee</strong> — the same man who signed her husband's certificate fifteen years later.
    One household, one family doctor.
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    The family's own descendant chart gives her as <em>Mary Taylor</em>, born England — which is where the
    <strong>Taylor</strong> in the MyHeritage tree's title comes from. <em>Septima</em>, "seventh", is an
    English Victorian name for a seventh child. Her maiden name is not yet documented here.
  </p>

  <h2>The will, and the three sons</h2>
  <p>
    The will was dated <strong>6 February 1942</strong>. The estate came to <strong>&pound;15,527 15s</strong>
    gross — a considerable sum in 1950 — leaving <strong>&pound;14,450 3s 7d</strong> for distribution in
    four equal shares of &pound;3,612 5s 11d.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Louis Lerena, major son</strong> — half a share.</li>
    <li><strong>Rose Lilian Theys, no relation</strong> — half a share. She was a spinster living at
      <strong>the same address</strong>, 7 Forth Road, and she was his executrix.</li>
    <li>The residue, <strong>&pound;10,837 17s 8d</strong>, held on trust for
      <strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena</strong> and <strong>Richardo Juan Carlos Lerena</strong>, major sons —
      the capital payable only <em>"when Nuno Fernando Lerena attains the age of thirty years"</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The succession duty addendum then supplies exact birth dates: <strong>Ricardo Juan Carlos Lerena, born
    6 January 1920</strong>, and <strong>Nuno Fernando Lerena, born 6 October 1924</strong> — who would turn
    thirty on 6 October 1954.
  </p>
  <div class="card" style="margin:1.2rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      <strong>That birth date is the join.</strong> Nuno Fernando Lerena, born 6 October 1924, is the man
      whose own death notice of 1971 names <strong>Cheryl Ann Lerena</strong> among his children. The line
      from Robert Paul Lerena to David Defranceski's mother is now continuous and documented at every step.
      <a href={u("/direct-line")}>The line &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The document that names generation 1</h2>
  <p>
    The estate account gives the sons but never the father's own parentage or origin. What identifies him is
    a <strong>death notice filed twenty-three years later</strong> — that of
    <strong>Roque Luis Armando Lerena</strong>, the "Louis" of the 1950 account, who died at Johannesburg on
    29 January 1973. Question 19 asks for the names of the deceased's parents:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Father: <strong>ROBERTO LERENA</strong> &middot; Mother: <strong>MARY SEPTIMA LERENA</strong> — both deceased
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Roberto</strong>, not Robert. The form also gives
    Roque's birth as <strong>6 December 1905 at Cape Town</strong>, his trade as
    <em>retired railwayman</em>, his first wife as <strong>Rosina Wilhelmina</strong> (predeceased) and his
    second as <strong>Rhena May</strong>, and his daughters as <strong>Juanita</strong> and
    <strong>Panola Maria</strong> — the "Juanita" and "Pam" of the family's own chart.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the family was in Cape Town by <strong>1905</strong> at the latest, and had begun having children
    there.
  </p>

  <h2>A detail worth keeping</h2>
  <p>
    Among the claims paid out of the estate, between the garage bill and the insurance premium, is one for
    <strong>&pound;26 to W. C. Blake — "claim for carrots"</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="small">
    A racehorse owner's last unpaid account. It is the smallest fact in this archive and one of the few that
    tells you what the yard smelled like.
  </p>

  <h2>What the estate does not say</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip">Open</span> Neither the estate nor Roque's death notice gives <strong>Roberto Paul
    Lerena's own parents</strong>, his exact birth date, or where in South America he was born. The
    <strong>death notice from his own 1950 file</strong> — which the account shows was prepared, and charged
    for, by the attorneys Dichmont &amp; Dichmont — would name them. It is not among the images held here.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</Base>
